window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] = window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] || {};
window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][11] = "<p>Days 1 to 3: use existing data. Orders with three or more items to one address at one time give a rough proxy for group orders: say 4% of orders (assumed). Read about 100 support tickets for coordination complaints.</p>\n<p>Days 3 to 8: eight interviews with customers who placed large orders recently, asking about the last time they ordered for a group, how they coordinated, what went wrong, and what they did about it.</p>\n<p>Days 6 to 12: a fake door. Show a &quot;Start a group order&quot; button to 3% of customers who recently ordered three or more items, leading to a &quot;coming soon, tell us what you need&quot; page with a short form.</p>\n<p>Days 10 to 14: a concierge test. Coordinate ten group orders by hand through messages.</p>\n<p>Before running anything, I'd write the criteria. At least five of eight interviewees describe a recent coordination struggle. At least 4% of the exposed click the fake door. At least seven of ten concierge orders complete with three or more participants. Rule: all three, build a thin version; two, iterate and retest; one or none, stop.</p>\n<p>The plan cannot tell us how group ordering performs at scale, or whether a built version would match a hand-run one. It tests the assumption most likely to be wrong, cheaply. I'd change the plan if the proxy shows group orders are well under 1% of orders: the opportunity would be too small to justify the time.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What can this two-week plan not tell you? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;It cannot show whether a shared cart would increase how often groups order, which needs a live test. It cannot show willingness to pay or the effect on restaurants, who might face larger, more complex orders. And eight interviews and a proxy from order data describe past behavior of people who already order, not people who avoid ordering for groups because it is hard. I would state those limits in the recommendation and propose the next step if the evidence is positive: a small pilot in one city with a shared-cart prototype and a measured change in group order frequency.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What would your decision rule be? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Written on day one, and the same as in the plan. Build the smallest version if all three hold: at least five of eight interviewees describe coordination pain in the last month, at least 4% of the exposed click the fake door, and at least seven of ten concierge orders complete with three or more participants. If two hold, iterate and retest, spending one more week on the test that missed or was borderline; if one or none holds, stop. The order-data proxy is a sanity check, not a criterion: if it shows group orders well under 1% of orders, I stop early. This keeps the decision to a rule I set before I saw the evidence, so that I cannot reinterpret it to fit what I hoped.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c06-06\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C06-06 · Hard · product design · DI</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Your interviews say users want a simpler interface, your survey says they want more features, and your analytics show most users use only three. How do you reconcile them?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Pick the one I trust most&quot; or &quot;average them.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks who each source covers, what each can and cannot show, and identifies the segments.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Sees that the sources may describe different segments, generates a testable hypothesis, and proposes a decision that serves each segment.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I wouldn't pick one source. I'd ask what each can and cannot show, and who it covers.</p>\n<p>The interviews show reasons, from a few people. Who did I recruit? If I interviewed beginners, &quot;simpler&quot; is natural. The survey shows what many say they want, which is stated, not behavior, and people often ask for more features while using few. The analytics show behavior across everyone: most use three features. That is consistent with a simpler interface being enough for most.</p>\n<p>The apparent conflict may be a segment difference. Perhaps most users are casual and need the three features to be easy, while a minority of heavy users want many features. I'd test it by splitting each source by usage level: do heavy users account for the &quot;more features&quot; answers in the survey, and are the interviewees casual users?</p>\n<p>Suppose the split shows that 80% of users use three features and most of the &quot;more features&quot; answers come from the heavy 10% (assumed). Then both sources are right about different groups. The hypothesis is that a simple default with an advanced mode serves both.</p>\n<p>My decision would be to simplify the default experience for the majority, since the behavior says that is where the volume is, and to offer progressive access to more features for heavy users, tested with a small group of each. The cost is added complexity of two modes. I would change my view if heavy users turn out to drive a large share of revenue or retention, in which case their needs weigh more than their numbers suggest.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you test whether the segments explain the conflict? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I would split users by usage from the analytics, say the top 10% by features used versus the rest, and join survey respondents to their usage by user ID. If the requests for more features come mostly from the heavy group and the interviewees who asked for simplicity are mostly in the casual group, the sources are describing different segments. Then I would interview five people from each group about their last week of work. The prediction is that casual users need the three features to be easier and heavy users need depth; if the heavy group also wants simplicity, the segment explanation is wrong and I would look elsewhere.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What decision would serve both groups? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;A simple default with progressive disclosure: the three common features are prominent and easy, and advanced features sit behind an Advanced section that heavy users can open or pin. I would measure task completion and time to first task for casual users, adoption of the advanced section by heavy users, and support contacts, with a guardrail that casual users' task time does not rise. If heavy users find the advanced section too hidden, I would let them promote features to the main view.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c06-07\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C06-07 · Hard · behavioral · BE</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Tell me about a time user research changed your mind.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> A story where the research confirmed what the candidate already thought.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> States the original belief, the method, what the evidence showed, and the decision that changed.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Shows how the candidate designed the research to be able to disprove the belief, how quickly they updated, the cost of the change, and what they now do differently.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> <em>Illustrative: a composite story; numbers invented.</em> &quot;I was the PM for a recipe app, and I believed that users would want a meal-planning calendar. It was my idea, and several colleagues liked it. Before building, I wrote down what would change my mind: if fewer than half of the people I interviewed planned meals at all, I would drop it.</p>\n<p>&quot;I interviewed ten regular users, asking about the last week of dinners. Only three planned meals in advance. The other seven decided that afternoon, usually while commuting, and the common frustration was not planning but 'what can I make with what's in my kitchen.' I also checked behavior: 61% of recipe views happened between 4pm and 6pm (invented).</p>\n<p>&quot;I dropped the calendar. I told the team the same day, with the numbers, and proposed a 'cook tonight' view that starts from the ingredients people have. It took us a week to prototype. In a test with 200 users, 38% of those who saw it cooked a recipe from it that evening against 22% for the standard home screen (invented).</p>\n<p>&quot;The cost was a month of discussion I had spent socializing the calendar idea, and the disappointment of a colleague who had designed screens for it. What I do differently now is write the disconfirming criterion before the first interview, and I show the team the research plan before the results, so that a change of direction does not feel like a loss for anyone.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What did changing your mind cost, and how did you handle the people who liked the idea? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;It cost about two weeks of design work and some disappointment, because three colleagues had championed the calendar. I handled it by showing my criterion from before the interviews (drop it if fewer than half plan meals) and the evidence: only three of ten planned, and 61% of recipe views came from the afternoon. I did not present it as the idea being wrong; I presented it as the research telling us where the need is. We then built a smaller feature around what is in my kitchen, and its first-month usage was higher than the calendar's projected usage.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What do you do differently now? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Three things. I write down what would change my mind before the research starts, so that I cannot move the goalposts. I recruit at least some participants who have no reason to flatter the idea, such as lapsed users. And I share raw quotes and behavior data with the team, not just my summary, so that others can see the evidence that changed my view and challenge my reading. The habit is to treat my own idea as a hypothesis with a stop condition.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c06-12-exercises\"></span>\n<h3>12. Exercises</h3>\n<p>Ten exercises. Each has a model solution and an alternative. Figures are assumptions unless marked.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c06-01\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C06-01 · Problem, solution or feature?</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Classify each statement as a problem, a solution class or a feature, and restate each request as a need: (a) &quot;Add a dark mode.&quot; (b) &quot;I can't read the screen at night without a headache.&quot; (c) &quot;We need push notifications.&quot; (d) &quot;Customers forget to complete their profile.&quot; (e) &quot;Build an AI assistant.&quot; (f) &quot;Let me filter by date.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A backlog contains these items from sales, users and executives.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None beyond the list.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> One line each.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Apply the three-layer ladder (FW-C06-01).</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) Feature (a specific implementation of reducing glare); need: reading comfortably in low light. (b) Problem. (c) Solution class (a way to reach people); need: reaching users who are not in the app, for something they care about. (d) Problem. (e) Solution class (very broad); need: unknown, ask what task people cannot do. (f) Feature; need: finding items from a time period (a search or navigation need).</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add a column with the first question you would ask each requester.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Reframing every item costs time; prioritize requests that recur or that are expensive to build.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c06-02\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C06-02 · Score three segments</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A budgeting app considers three segments: (A) recent graduates with student loans, (B) freelancers with irregular income, (C) couples sharing expenses. Score them and choose one.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The company has a small team and an app with basic budgeting.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> <em>Invented.</em> Size: A 8 million, B 12 million, C 20 million. Pain intensity from interviews: A medium, B high (income swings), C medium. Reach: A high through campuses and social, B medium, C low (hard to target). Fit: B high because the app's forecasting could be adapted, A medium, C low.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> State the criterion and what you give up.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C06-02.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> B has the highest pain and a strong fit, with medium reach; C is largest but hard to reach and a poor fit; A is easiest to reach but with medium pain. Choose B (freelancers). Criterion: pain intensity combined with fit. Give up C's size and A's reach. Next evidence: eight interviews with freelancers about the last month when income was late, and a landing page offering an irregular-income forecast.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Weight criteria (pain 40%, fit 30%, reach 20%, size 10%) and score numerically; use it to break ties, not to decide.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A narrower segment means a smaller early market, which a later expansion plan must address.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c06-03\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C06-03 · Write job statements</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Write a job statement (&quot;When..., I want to..., so I can...&quot;) for each vignette: (a) a nurse switching shifts who wants to swap with a colleague; (b) a student with three deadlines in one week; (c) a small-shop owner who gets paid by many customers in different ways.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> You are preparing for interviews.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Include a functional, emotional or social element in each.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C06-03.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) &quot;When I need a different shift, I want to find a colleague who can swap and have it approved quickly, so I can plan my week without chasing people (and without looking unreliable to my manager).&quot; (b) &quot;When several deadlines collide, I want to see what to do first and how long each will take, so I can avoid an all-night scramble and feel in control.&quot; (c) &quot;When payments arrive in cash, by card and by transfer, I want one clear view of what I was paid today, so I can close the day with confidence that the numbers are right.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Write the job for the person who competes with your product (a group chat, a spreadsheet).</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Specific statements guide features and may exclude users; use several jobs for several segments.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c06-04\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C06-04 · Fix the interview questions</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Rewrite each question to remove the flaw: (a) &quot;Would you use an app that reminds you to drink water?&quot; (b) &quot;Don't you find it annoying when ads interrupt videos?&quot; (c) &quot;How often do you exercise?&quot; (d) &quot;What features would you like us to add?&quot; (e) &quot;How much would you pay for a better calendar?&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> You are preparing an interview guide.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Ask about the past and about specifics.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> The rules in C06.5 and FW-C06-04.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) &quot;Tell me about the last time you realized you hadn't had enough water. What happened?&quot; (b) &quot;Tell me about the last time an ad interrupted a video. What did you do?&quot; (c) &quot;Walk me through your last week: when did you exercise, if at all, and what got in the way on the other days?&quot; (d) &quot;Tell me about the last time the product didn't do something you needed. What did you do instead?&quot; (e) &quot;What do you use to manage your schedule today? Tell me about the last time it went wrong. What did that cost you?&quot; The price question comes later, as a behavioral test, not as an opinion.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add a follow-up to each (&quot;show me,&quot; &quot;what happened next?&quot;).</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Past-behavior questions take longer to answer and yield stronger evidence.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c06-05\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C06-05 · Margin of error and sample size</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> (a) A survey of 100 users finds 40% want a feature. What is the approximate 95% margin of error? (b) How many responses do you need for ±5 points, ±3 points and ±2 points in the worst case? (c) 400 respondents split 52% to 48%. Can you act on it?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Planning a survey.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Use the rule: margin ≈ 1 ÷ the square root of n (worst case, about 0.98 ÷ the square root of n).</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Show the arithmetic.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Apply the rule and its inverse.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) For 40% with n = 100: 1.96 × the square root of (0.4 × 0.6 ÷ 100) = 1.96 × 0.049 = about 9.6 points, so 30% to 50%. (b) n = (0.98 ÷ margin)²: ±5 points gives about 384 responses; ±3 gives about 1,067; ±2 gives about 2,401. (c) With n = 400 the margin is about ±5 points, so 52% versus 48% is within noise (a 4-point gap with a ±5 margin on each); you cannot say which is larger.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Reduce the required sample by accepting a wider margin, or by focusing on a segment where larger effects are expected.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Larger samples cost time and money; the margin shrinks slowly (quadrupling the sample halves it).</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c06-06\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C06-06 · Stated versus actual</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A survey of 800 users (assumed) says 40% would use a new &quot;smart reminders&quot; feature. A fake-door test shows it to 6,000 users and 5% click &quot;turn on,&quot; then only 40% of clickers keep it on after a week. Compute the likely adoption and the stated-to-actual ratio.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Forecasting for a planning meeting. The app has 200,000 monthly active users.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Give a forecast with a rough range.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Convert funnel steps to a behavior-based rate.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Click: 5% of 6,000 = 300. Keep on after a week: 40% of 300 = 120, which is 2% of the exposed (120 ÷ 6,000). Stated: 40%. Ratio: 40% ÷ 2% = 20. Forecast for 200,000 users: 2% = about 4,000 users keeping the feature on, against 80,000 implied by the survey. Margin on the 2%: 1.96 × the square root of (0.02 × 0.98 ÷ 6,000) = about 0.35 points, so 1.65% to 2.35%, or about 3,300 to 4,700 users.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Use the click rate alone (5%, 10,000 users) as an upper bound and the retained rate as the working number.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A one-week retention check delays the answer and prevents over-counting curiosity clicks.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c06-07\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C06-07 · Weigh the feedback</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Four pieces of feedback: (1) a social-media thread with 300 likes demanding a &quot;dark theme&quot;; (2) 40 support tickets in a month about sync failures; (3) three enterprise sales losses citing missing single sign-on; (4) interviews in which 6 of 8 users describe copying data by hand between two screens. Rank them using FW-C06-06.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Planning a quarter for a project-management tool.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> <em>Invented:</em> the thread comes from non-customers as far as you can tell; 40 tickets come from 31 distinct accounts; the three losses were all large accounts; interviews were with target-segment users.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> State confidence and the next check.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Weigh by segment, source consistency, frequency and severity.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (2) Sync failures: many accounts, high severity (data loss risk), behavior-based; confidence high; check data on sync errors. (4) Manual copying: target segment, consistent in interviews; confidence medium; check how many users do it with behavior data. (3) Single sign-on: few losses, but large accounts and a possible requirement; confidence medium; check the pipeline's share requiring it. (1) Dark theme: loud, non-customer source, one channel; confidence low; check with a small survey of customers. The rule: a theme in several independent sources and in the target segment outranks a loud single source.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add a cost column and rank by value over effort.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Weighting by revenue can overlook small customers who would grow; keep one slot for experiments.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c06-08\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C06-08 · Rewrite weak problem statements</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Rewrite each into a strong problem statement: (a) &quot;We need to make onboarding better.&quot; (b) &quot;Users want more notifications.&quot; (c) &quot;The search is bad.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The team backlog has these as titles.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> <em>Invented:</em> (a) 38% of small-team admins finish setup in a week, against 70% for large teams; (b) 20 users in a forum asked for more alerts, while 12% of users turned off notifications last month; (c) 14% of searches end with no click and a reformulated query within 10 seconds.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> No solution words; include segment, behavior, impact and evidence.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> The five-row template (FW-C02-01) and the tests in C06.8.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) &quot;Admins at companies with fewer than 50 people stop at the integration step during setup; 38% finish within a week against 70% at larger companies; evidence: funnel data and four interviews.&quot; (b) &quot;Some users want timely alerts about specific events, while 12% of users turned notifications off last month; unclear which alerts the 20 forum users want and whether they are typical; evidence: low, a forum thread and settings data.&quot; (c) &quot;In 14% of searches, users reformulate within ten seconds without clicking a result, which suggests the first results did not match what they wanted; evidence: search logs; segment unknown.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add a &quot;not the problem&quot; line to each (for example, &quot;not 'add a wizard'&quot;).</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Good statements take more words and prevent building the wrong thing.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c06-09\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C06-09 · Riskiest assumption and cheapest test</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Idea: a marketplace that connects pet owners with neighbors who will pet-sit. List assumptions, pick the riskiest, and design a test with a criterion.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A team of two, three weeks, no budget.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> The test must measure behavior, not opinion.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C06-07.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Assumptions: owners want to leave pets with neighbors, not kennels (desirability, high importance, high uncertainty); neighbors are willing and trustworthy (desirability, high, high); price works (viability, medium); matching is feasible (low risk). The riskiest is the two-sided desirability: owners trust a neighbor with their pet. Test: a landing page for one neighborhood with a &quot;request a sitter&quot; form and a recruiting post for sitters; concierge-match ten requests by hand. Criteria: at least 20 owner requests and at least 10 sitter sign-ups in two weeks; at least 6 of 10 concierge matches completed and rated positively. Decision: all met, build a simple matching tool; fewer, drop or change the audience.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Interview ten pet owners about the last time they traveled, and who looked after the pet.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A one-neighborhood test is quick and may not generalize to other places.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c06-10\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C06-10 · Eight-minute research plan drill</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> In eight minutes, plan the research for: &quot;Why do new users of our expense-tracking app stop after the first week?&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A timed spoken drill using the Type A structure.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None given.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Decision, segments, hypotheses, methods in order, biases, decision rule.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Section 4, Type A.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Decision: whether to change onboarding, add reminders, or neither. Segments: by acquisition source and by whether they logged a second expense. Hypotheses: they never log a second expense because the first was hard (usability); they do not see a reason to return (value); the app does not fit how they spend (need). Methods: behavioral data first (funnel from install to the second expense, by source), then eight interviews with people who stopped, asking them to walk through the last week, then a short survey to size the reasons, and a check of behavior by reason. Biases: lapsed users are hard to recruit, so I'd offer a small incentive and recruit through email; interviewees who answer may be the more helpful ones. Decision rule: if most drop before the second expense, work on first-use; if many logged several and left, work on value and reminders. Cannot tell: whether a fix raises retention without a test.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Start with five session recordings of first-week behavior to generate hypotheses.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Eight minutes allows a plan, not details; say what you would add with more time.</p>\n<span id=\"c06-13-practice-protocol\"></span>\n<h3>13. Practice protocol</h3>\n<p><strong>Timed drills (three a week for three weeks).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Week 1: reframe and segment.</em> Take a feature request (the exercises and IQ-C06-01 and IQ-C06-02), step up the ladder, write the problem statement and name the segment, in five minutes.</li>\n<li><em>Week 2: the research plan.</em> Use the ten-minute budget in section 4 on a Type A prompt (IQ-C06-03, IQ-C06-05, EX-C06-10), speaking aloud or recording.</li>\n<li><em>Week 3: the evidence.</em> Ask a partner to give conflicting data (&quot;interviews say X, analytics say Y&quot;) and to challenge one claim with &quot;how do you know?&quot; Update aloud.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Interview practice (twice).</strong> Run two real 20-minute interviews with people in a segment you care about, following FW-C06-04. Record them with permission. Review: how many of your questions asked about the past, how many were leading, and how long you talked.</p>\n<p><strong>Feedback checklist (yes, partly, no).</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Did I separate the problem, the solution and the feature?</li>\n<li>Did I segment by need and behavior and name a criterion?</li>\n<li>Did I state the decision the research informs?</li>\n<li>Did I name my riskiest assumption?</li>\n<li>Did I choose methods in an order, with a reason?</li>\n<li>Did I name biases specific to my plan?</li>\n<li>Did I prefer behavior to stated intention and use a commitment test where relevant?</li>\n<li>Did I write a problem statement without solution words?</li>\n<li>Did I write a decision rule before the test?</li>\n<li>Did I say what the research cannot tell me?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>With a partner.</strong> Ask your partner to answer as a user who says &quot;yes, I'd definitely use that.&quot; Practice asking about the last time, and notice how the answer changes.</p>\n<p><strong>Answer journal.</strong> For each drill record the date, the prompt, the segment, the riskiest assumption, your score out of ten, and the question you wish you had asked. After six drills, count how often you reached for a survey first; if it is most of the time, drill the interview-first order.</p>\n<span id=\"c06-14-implementation-playbook\"></span>\n<h3>14. Implementation playbook</h3>\n<p>Apply the chapter on real work over the next two weeks. Each deliverable doubles as an interview story.</p>\n<p><strong>Days 1 to 3.</strong> Take the three most recent feature requests you received. For each, step up the ladder (FW-C06-01): write the request, the need, the workaround and the problem, and confirm it with the requester.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> Run three user interviews with the guide in FW-C06-04, each about the last time a person did a task related to your product. Write up the themes with counts, and note one thing that surprised you.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> Write one problem statement using the template and the six tests in C06.8, and share it with your manager and an engineer. Ask whether it names the problem without the solution.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Choose one assumption behind a current project, rate its importance and uncertainty, and design the cheapest behavioral test with a criterion written in advance (FW-C06-07). Run it if you can.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Build a feedback weighting table (FW-C06-06) for the last month's feedback, and compare it with the loudest request.</p>\n<p><strong>Deliverables that become stories:</strong> the interview themes (a time you learned something that changed a decision), the problem statement (aligning a team on the problem), and the test with a pre-set criterion (a decision made on evidence). Keep the numbers real.</p>\n<span id=\"c06-15-key-takeaways-and-further-reading\"></span>\n<h3>15. Key takeaways and further reading</h3>\n<span id=\"c06-key-takeaways\"></span>\n<h4>Key takeaways</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Separate the problem from the solution and the feature, and step up from a request to what the person is trying to do.</li>\n<li>Segment by need and behavior, choose by a stated criterion, and say what you give up.</li>\n<li>Use qualitative research to find, quantitative research to size, and behavior to check; prefer what people did to what they say they will do.</li>\n<li>Write the problem so a team can act on it without naming the solution, with the evidence and its strength.</li>\n<li>Test the riskiest assumption cheaply, with the criterion written first and a decision for each outcome.</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c06-further-reading\"></span>\n<h4>Further reading</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Clayton M. Christensen, Taddy Hall, Karen Dillon and David S. Duncan, <em>Competing Against Luck</em> (2016). The jobs-to-be-done perspective; and their article &quot;Know Your Customers' 'Jobs to Be Done'&quot; (<em>Harvard Business Review</em>, September 2016) <code>[V: Competing Against Luck: HarperBusiness, 4 October 2016, ISBN 9780062435613; the companion article is Harvard Business Review, September 2016]</code>.</li>\n<li>Rob Fitzpatrick, <em>The Mom Test</em> (2013). Asking about the past instead of the future, and avoiding polite lies.</li>\n<li>Teresa Torres, <em>Continuous Discovery Habits</em> (2021). Regular customer contact, opportunity mapping and assumption testing.</li>\n<li>Steve Portigal, <em>Interviewing Users</em> (2nd edition, 2023). A practical guide to the craft of user interviews.</li>\n<li>Erika Hall, <em>Just Enough Research</em> (2nd edition, 2019). A short guide to choosing research methods.</li>\n<li>Rahul Vohra, &quot;How Superhuman Built an Engine to Find Product/Market Fit,&quot; First Round Review, 12 November 2018 (review.firstround.com/how-superhuman-built-an-engine-to-find-product-market-fit/). The source for CS-C06-02 and FW-C06-08.</li>\n<li>Marty Cagan, <em>Inspired</em> (2nd edition, 2017). Product discovery as an ongoing discipline.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"part-iii\"></span>\n";
